     }
    ],
    "etymology": {
     "text": "Cognates such as Aramaic מָה (mā, “what”), Arabic مَا (mā, “what”) and Ugaritic 𐎎𐎅 (mh) can be found across the Central Semitic languages.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "The Passover Seder’s Four Questions open with מַה נִּשְׁתַּנָּה הַלַּיְלָה הַזֶּה — “What makes this night different?”"
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "No verb “is” is needed: מָה זֶה? = What is this?",
     "“Which …?” is usually אֵיזֶה / אֵיזוֹ, not מָה: אֵיזֶה יָרָק? (which vegetable?)."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "מָה alone (“What?!”) can express surprise or impatience, depending on the tone."
    ]
